          "prompt": "Stylized hand-painted 3D game environment art for a fast first-person roguelite shooter, matching the\nreadable visual direction of Gunfire Reborn: bold chunky silhouettes, broad clean faceted planes, two or three dominant\ncolour blocks, soft painted gradients and a few restrained edge accents. East-Asian Silk Road frontier fantasy of the Han-dynasty Gobi desert: ochre-red sandstone and wind-carved yardang rock with horizontal strata, pale sand-yellow rammed earth with reed layers, weathered grey-brown timber, and only a few faded Dunhuang mural accents in malachite green, lapis blue and vermilion. Matte stone,\nwood and cloth, restrained highlights on metal, sparse painted wear. No photorealism, micro-grain, dense tiny cracks,\nelaborate miniature carvings, harsh specular highlights or baked cast shadows. Even neutral lighting.\n\nAsset (2.6m wide x 5.5m tall x 2.6m deep): The spire (chattra finial) of a buried Silk Road stupa sticking up out of the sand, 5.5m tall and 2.6m wide: at the bottom a chunky square mud-brick base block (harmika) 2.6m wide and 1.5m tall with one thin plain cornice, ochre-red with a faded vermilion band; on it a thick central mast carrying seven stacked round parasol discs that taper from 2.2m wide at the bottom to 0.9m at the top, each disc a thick chunky ring with rounded edges and an even gap between rings; on top a fat lotus-bud jewel finial. The discs and jewel are tarnished dark gilt bronze with warm gold highlights and patches of verdigris green; one disc is slightly tilted for a weathered look. PROPORTIONS: all four views are identical, drawn about 2.1 times as tall as wide, a tapering stack like a chunky tiered parasol tower. Broad faceted hand-painted planes, chunky forms, no hanging bells, chains, thin spikes or ornaments. One solid connected piece on a flat base.\n\nOutput: one orthographic model turnaround sheet for 3D reconstruction. Exactly four separate complete views\nof the SAME object in one row, left to right:\n1. FRONT view: the object's front faces the viewer.\n2. LEFT side view: the object turned 90 degrees so its front points to the LEFT edge of the image (camera at the\n   object's right-hand side).\n3. BACK view: the object's back faces the viewer.\n4. RIGHT side view: the object turned 90 degrees so its front points to the RIGHT edge of the image.\nSame scale and same ground line in all four, each centred in its quarter with generous empty margin, never touching or\noverlapping; the whole object fits inside its quarter, nothing cropped.\nPlain flat light neutral grey background (#D6D6D6). No floor, no ground shadow, no text, no labels, no frames, no\nperspective.",
